      "questionTextEng": "Suppose the government has decided to provide a per-unit subsidy to local hotels and, at the same time, staying in\n      local hotels for vacation has become popular since the Outbound Travel Alert on all overseas territories was\n      issued. If the supply is not perfectly inelastic, which of the following statements about the local hotel market is\n      CORRECT?\n\n            A.    Consumers’ total expenditure on local hotel room rentals will increase.\n            B.    The market price of local hotel rooms will decrease.\n            C.    The deadweight loss will increase.\n            D.    The producer surplus will decrease.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    When the government provides a per-unit subsidy to hotels, the supply of hotel rooms will increase. The\n    demand for hotel rooms will increase as staying in local hotels for vacation becomes popular.\n    Without the government subsidy, there is no deadweight loss. Providing a per-unit subsidy will result in\n    a deadweight loss.\n    Option A: As both supply and demand increase, the quantity transacted will increase while the change in\n    the market price will be uncertain. The change in consumers’ total expenditure on local hotel rooms will\n    thus be uncertain.\n    Option B: As both supply and demand increase, the change in the market price will be uncertain.\n    Option D: Both the provision of a per-unit subsidy and an increase in demand would increase the\n    producer surplus.",
