      "questionTextEng": "Kevin is making plans for the summer holidays. He has several options and his order of preference is shown\n     below:\n\n          First option: to learn to drive a car\n          Second option: to work as an intern at a TV station\n          Third option: to work as a research assistant in a university\n\n     Suppose Kevin’s order of preference remains unchanged. Which of the following will lower Kevin’s opportunity\n     cost of working as an intern at a TV station?\n\n           A.    Kevin finds that he does not enjoy driving a car.\n           B.    The requirements for Hong Kong full driving licence holders to apply for an International Driving\n                 Permit are relaxed.\n           C.    The TV station reduces the salary for interns.\n           D.    The market demand for people specialised in the subject in which Kevin works as a research assistant\n                 in the university increases.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n     Suppose Kevin’s order of preference remains unchanged. Kevin’s opportunity cost of working as an\n     intern at a TV station (the highest-valued option forgone) is the first option (to learn to drive a car).\n     Option A: This will reduce the value of the first option. The opportunity cost of working as an intern at a\n     TV station will decrease.\n     Option B: This will increase the value of the first option. The opportunity cost of working as an intern at\n     a TV station will increase.\n     Option C: This will reduce the value of the chosen option. The opportunity cost of working as an intern\n     at a TV station will not be affected.\n     Option D: This is not the highest-valued option forgone, so the increase in its value will not reduce the\n     opportunity cost of working as an intern at a TV station.",
