      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    As offenders need to pay a fine, the fine increases the marginal private cost of smoking.\n    Option A: As the fine may not be equal to the marginal external cost, the quantity of cases of smoking\n    may still be higher than the socially optimal level.\n    Option C: The fine is a government solution to the divergence between private and social costs.\n    Option D: Even if the fine reflects the external cost of smoking accurately, and the quantity of cases of\n    smoking reduces to the socially optimal level which is lower than the equilibrium quantity, there may\n    still be people smoking.",
