      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/29/q-p1-01.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/29/a-p1-01.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Jason is planning to use the electronic consumption vouchers issued by the government for consumption. He has\n     several options and his order of preference is as follows.\n\n     First option: to buy a mountain bike\n     Second option: to have a buffet dinner at a hotel\n     Third option: to buy a smartphone\n\n     Which of the following factors would LEAST likely affect the cost-benefit analysis for Jason’s consumption\n     choice?\n\n           A.    As a result of the celebrity effect, the resale price of the mountain bike increases sharply.\n           B.    The hotel offers discounts for buffet dinners.\n           C.    The functions of the smartphone are not as good as Jason expects.\n           D.    More freebies are given when purchasing the smartphone.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n     Jason would choose the first option as the benefit of choosing the first option (the value of the first\n     option) is higher than the cost (the value of the second option).\n     Option C is correct. This would reduce the value of the third option. This would not affect Jason’s cost\n     or benefit of choosing the first option.\n     Option A is incorrect. This would increase the value of the first option, thus increasing Jason’s benefit of\n     choosing the first option.\n     Option B is incorrect. This would increase the value of the second option, thus increasing Jason’s cost of\n     choosing the first option.\n     Option D is incorrect. This may increase the value of the third option to a level higher than that of the\n     second option, so the cost of choosing the first option may change from the value of the second option\n     to that of the third option.",
