      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows the cost-output relationship of a price taker.\n\n                 Output (units)                        3           4              5         6          7\n                 Average cost ($)                      20          21             22        23         24\n\n      If the average cost of every unit of output increases by $1 and the market price is $27,\n\n      (1)   the profit-maximising output is 5 units.\n      (2)   the marginal cost of every unit of output will increase by $1.\n      (3)   the maximum profit is $20.\n\n            A.     (1) and (2) only\n            B.     (1) and (3) only\n            C.     (2) and (3) only\n            D.     (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n              Output (units)                       3           4          5            6         7\n              Original average cost ($)            20          21         22          23        24\n              Total cost ($)                       60          84         110         138      168\n              Marginal cost ($)                   -         24            26          28        30\n     After the average cost of every unit of output increases by $1:\n              Output (units)                      3          4            5            6         7\n              Average cost ($)                     21          22         23          24        25\n              Total cost ($)                       63          88         115         144      175\n              Marginal cost ($)                     -          25         27          29        31\n     When the market price is $27, the profit-maximising output is 5 units (where marginal cost = marginal\n    revenue = price).\n    When the output is 5 units, total cost is $115. Profit = $27 × 5 − $115 = $20.",
