      "questionTextEng": "An economy initially has an inflationary (output) gap. Which of the following would NOT help the economy\n      narrow the inflationary (output) gap?\n\n           A.    The domestic currency appreciates.\n           B.    The government raises the business registration fee.\n           C.    The price of raw materials falls.\n           D.    The government cancels the production subsidies provided to firms.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    The decrease in the price of raw materials will increase short run aggregate supply. The output level will\n    increase and the inflationary (output) gap will be widened.\n    Option A is incorrect.When the domestic currency appreciates, the relative price of its exports will\n    increase and the relative price of its imports will decrease, resulting in a decrease in exports and an\n    increase in imports. Net exports will decrease and aggregate demand will decrease. The output level will\n    decrease, helping the economy narrow the inflationary (output) gap.\n    Option B is incorrect. When the government raises the business registration fee, investment expenditure\n    will decrease. Aggregate demand will decrease. The output level will decrease, helping the economy\n    narrow the inflationary (output) gap.\n    Option D is incorrect. When the government cancels the production subsidies provided to firms, the\n    production cost of firms will increase. Short run aggregate supply will decrease. The output level will\n    decrease, helping the economy narrow the inflationary (output) gap.",
