      "questionTextEng": "The money supply of an economy decreased while its monetary base remained unchanged. Which of the\n      following could explain the above situation?\n\n       (1) The central bank raised the discount rate.\n       (2) The central bank raised the required reserve ratio.\n       (3) The central bank sold government bonds to the public.\n\n           A.    (1) only\n           B.    (2) only\n           C.    (1) and (3) only\n           D.    (2) and (3) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     Monetary base = Cash in public circulation + Bank reserves\n     Money supply = Cash in public circulation + Bank deposits\n     (2) is correct. When the central bank raised the required reserve ratio, banks had to call back some of\n     their loans from debtors or sell some of their assets to the public in order to fulfil the reserve\n     requirement. Deposits would decrease and the money supply would decrease through deposit\n     contraction. Raising the required reserve ratio would not affect reserves or cash in public circulation, so\n     the monetary base would remain unchanged.\n     (1) is incorrect. When the central bank raised the discount rate, the money supply would decrease.\n     Commercial banks had to borrow money from the central bank at a higher cost. The reserves of\n     commercial banks would decrease and the monetary base would decrease.\n     (3) is incorrect. When the central bank sold government bonds to the public, cash in public circulation\n     or bank deposits would be used to buy bonds. Both the monetary base and the money supply would\n    decrease.",
