      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/29/a-p1-38.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The following table shows a change in the salaries tax structure of an economy.\n\n                                                            Taxable income ($)\n                  Average tax rate             Old bracket                     New bracket\n                        2%             Less than or equal to 50,000    Less than or equal to 60,000\n                        5%                    50,001-70,000                   60,001-80,000\n                       10%                    70,001-90,000                   80,001-90,000\n                       15%                   90,001-110,000                    Unchanged\n                       20%                Greater than 110,000                 Unchanged\n\n      Suppose the basic tax allowance remains unchanged. Compared to the old tax structure, the new tax structure will\n\n       (1) narrow the tax base.\n       (2) narrow the income gap between the rich and the poor.\n       (3) reduce the total tax revenue.\n\n           A.    (2) only\n           B.    (1) and (2) only\n           C.    (1) and (3) only\n           D.    (2) and (3) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    (2) is correct. The average tax rate paid by low-income earners decreases. For example, if someone\n    earns a taxable income of $60,000, he paid an average tax rate of 5% in the past and he pays an average\n    tax rate of 2% now. At the same time, the average tax rate paid by high-income earners remains\n    unchanged. The income gap between the rich and the poor will thus be narrowed.\n    (1) is incorrect. As the basic tax allowance remains unchanged, the tax base will remain unchanged.\n    (3) is incorrect. As the average tax rate paid by low-income earners decreases, their work incentive may\n    increase, causing an increase in taxable income. The increase in tax revenue due to the increase in\n    taxable income may be greater than the decrease in tax revenue due to the decrease in the tax rate, so the\n    change in total tax revenue will be uncertain.",
