      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/29/q-p1-40.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/29/a-p1-40.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following diagram.\n\n                          Price level\n                                            LRAS\n\n                                                           SRAS\n\n                                                   E\n                                                A\n\n                                                            AD\n\n                                                                   Aggregate output\n                           0                  Y1\n\n      The long run equilibrium point of an economy changes from E to A. Which of the following is a possible reason\n      for the change?\n\n            A.    The government lowers the discount rate.\n            B.    The government raises the import tariff.\n            C.    The transportation cost for raw materials decreases.\n            D.    People’s desire to save increases.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    When aggregate demand decreases, the long run equilibrium point will shift from E to A.\n    Option D is correct. An increase in people’s desire to save will lead to a decrease in private consumption\n    expenditure. Aggregate demand will decrease. There will be a deflationary (output) gap in the economy.\n    There will be excess supply in the factor market, resulting in a downward pressure on input prices. The\n    cost of production will decrease. Short run aggregate supply will increase. In the long run, the output\n    level will return to the full-employment output level (Y1).\n    Option A is incorrect. When the government lowers the discount rate, the money supply will increase\n    and the interest rate will decrease. Consumption and investment will increase. Aggregate demand will\n    increase.\n    Option B is incorrect. When the government raises the import tariffs, imports will decrease. Net exports\n    will increase and aggregate demand will increase.\n    Option C is incorrect. A decrease in the transportation cost for raw materials will result in an increase in\n    short run aggregate supply without affecting aggregate demand.",
