      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    Average labour productivity in year 1 = 600 units of output / (15 × 8 man-hours)\n     = 5 units of output per man-hour\n     Average labour productivity in year 2 = 675 units output / (20 × 7.5 man-hours)\n     = 4.5 units of output per man-hour\n     The firm’s average labour productivity decreased.\n     If the firm does not provide adequate training for new workers, average labour productivity may\n     decrease.\n     Option A is incorrect. If worker morale improves, average labour productivity will increase.\n     Option B is incorrect. If the firm uses more capital goods, workers can produce more output with the\n     same number of working hours, and average labour productivity will increase.\n     Option D is incorrect. If the wage payment method is changed from time rate to piece rate, workers’\n     incentive to work will increase and average labour productivity will increase.",
