      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/30/a-p1-20.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Good X and good Y are in joint supply. When the price of good Y decreases, the equilibrium price of good X\n      changes by 15%, while the quantity transacted decreases from 210 units to 190 units.\n\n      Which of the following best explains the above change?\n\n            A.    Good X has many close substitutes.\n            B.    There are many barriers for firms to enter the market of good X.\n            C.    Firms producing good X have spare production capacity.\n            D.    Good X is a habit-forming good.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    Given that good X and good Y are in joint supply. When the price of good Y decreases, the supply of\n    good X will decrease. The price will increase and the quantity transacted will decrease.\n    Percentage decrease in quantity transacted = (190 ‒ 210) / [(210 + 190) / 2] × 100% = 10%\n    As the percentage change in quantity demanded (10%) is smaller than the percentage change in price\n    (15%), the demand for good X is inelastic.\n    If good X is a habit-forming good, the demand for good X tends to be less elastic.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/30/ae-p1-20.jpg",
      "originalQuestionImageEng": "originals/30/qe-p1-20.jpg",
