      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/31/q-p1-10.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/31/a-p1-10.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows the cost-output relationship of a price taker.\n\n                            Output (units)                  Total cost ($)\n                                 0                                5\n                                 1                               10\n                                 2                               22\n                                 3                               36\n                                 4                               52\n                                 5                               70\n\n      If the market price of the good is $14, which of the following statements are CORRECT?\n\n            (1)   At the profit-maximising output level, the total producer surplus is $11.\n            (2)   At the profit-maximising output level, the profit is $6.\n            (3)   The law of diminishing marginal returns applies to the firm.\n            (4)   The firm operates in the long run.\n\n            A.    (1) and (2) only\n            B.    (1) and (4) only\n            C.    (1), (2) and (3) only\n            D.    (2), (3) and (4) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n                           Output (units)          Total cost ($)   Marginal cost ($)\n                                  0                      5                   -\n                                  1                     10                   5\n                                  2                     22                  12\n                                  3                     36                  14\n                                  4                     52                  16\n                                  5                     70                  18\n     If the market price of the good is $14, the output is 3 units, because at this output level, the marginal\n     revenue is equal to the marginal cost.\n     (1) is correct. Total producer surplus = $[(14  5) + (14  12) + (14  14)] = $11.\n     (2) is correct. Profit = ($14 × 3)–$36 = $6.\n     (3) is correct. Marginal cost reflects the change in variable cost. An increase in marginal cost means an\n     increasing amount of a variable factor is required to increase output by 1 unit. That indicates a fall in the\n     marginal productivity of a variable factor. As marginal cost keeps on rising, it implies that marginal\n     product keeps on falling. The law of diminishing marginal returns applies to the firm.\n     (4) is incorrect. The firm operates in the short run as there is a fixed cost.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/31/ae-p1-10.jpg",
