      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/31/q-p1-23.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/31/a-p1-23.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following proposals will reduce the Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social transfer monthly\n      household income in Hong Kong?\n\n           (1)   increasing the charge on plastic shopping bags\n           (2)   increasing the marginal tax rates for the salaries tax\n           (3)   waiving the rent for the elderly living in public rental housing units\n\n           A.    (1) and (2) only\n           B.    (1) and (3) only\n           C.    (2) and (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    (2) is correct. Increasing the marginal tax rates of the salaries tax will make high-income earners pay\n    more tax while low-income earners will not be affected. The income gap between the rich and the poor\n    will be narrowed and the Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social transfer monthly household\n    income will decrease.\n    (3) is correct. The elderly living in public housing units are generally low-income people. If the\n    government waives their rent, their income after taxes and social transfers will increase while\n    high-income people will not be affected, and the income gap between the rich and the poor will be\n    narrowed. The Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social transfer monthly household income will\n    decrease.\n\n    (1) is incorrect. Since everyone faces the same plastic shopping bag charge, the increase in the charge\n    will take up a higher proportion of the income of low-income people than high-income people, and the\n    income gap between the rich and the poor will be widened. The Gini coefficient based on post-tax\n    post-social transfer monthly household income will increase.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/31/ae-p1-23.jpg",
      "originalQuestionImageEng": "originals/31/qe-p1-23.jpg",
