      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/31/q-p1-25.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/31/a-p1-25.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Consider the following statistical data about Country A.\n\n                                                             20X1                         20X2\n                  Change in nominal GDP                      ‒5%                          ‒2%\n                  Change in real GDP                          1%                           1%\n                  Change in population size                   0%                          ‒3%\n\n      Which of the following statements about Country A is/are CORRECT?\n\n            (1)    Per capita nominal GDP increased in 20X2.\n            (2)    Real GDP in 20X2 remained unchanged.\n            (3)    The general price level decreased in 20X2.\n\n            A.     (1) only\n            B.     (1) and (3) only\n            C.     (2) and (3) only\n            D.     (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    (1) is correct. Per capita nominal GDP = Nominal GDP / Population size\n    In 20X2, the percentage change in nominal GDP was ‒2%, while the percentage change in population\n    size was ‒3%. Since the percentage decrease in nominal GDP was smaller than the percentage decrease\n    in population size, per capita nominal GDP increased in 20X2.\n    (3) is correct. Implicit price deflator of GDP = (Nominal GDP / Real GDP) × 100\n    In 20X2, nominal GDP decreased (percentage change = ‒2%), while real GDP increased (percentage\n    change = 1%), implying that the general price level decreased.\n    (2) is incorrect. In 20X2, the growth rate of real GDP was 1%, reflecting an increase in real GDP.",
