      "inlineDiagrams": "diagrams/MT31-P1-29.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following diagram.\n\n                           Price level\n                                              LRAS\n\n                                                              SRAS\n                                                    A\n\n                                                    E\n\n                                                             AD\n\n                              0                                        Aggregate output\n                                                Y\n\n      The initial equilibrium point of Country A is at E. If the new long run equilibrium point is at A, which of the\n      following is a possible reason for the change in the equilibrium point?\n\n            A.    A large number of factories are temporarily closed due to a labour strike.\n            B.    The central bank prints more money.\n            C.    The stock market in Country A’s largest export market declines.\n            D.    The transportation cost for raw materials decreases.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    The central bank printing more money will cause the money supply to increase and the interest rate will\n    decrease. Consumption and investment will increase. Aggregate demand will increase, resulting in an\n    inflationary (output) gap. There will be excess demand in the factor market, resulting in an upward\n    pressure on input prices. The cost of production will increase. Short run aggregate supply will decrease.\n    In the long run, the price level at the new equilibrium point will be higher than that at the original\n    equilibrium point, while the output level will return to the full-employment output level.\n    Option A is incorrect. The temporary closure of a large number of factories due to a labour strike will\n    reduce short run aggregate supply, resulting in a deflationary (output) gap. There will be excess supply\n    in the factor market, resulting in a downward pressure on input prices. The cost of production will\n    decrease. Short run aggregate supply will increase. In the long run, the new equilibrium point will be\n    equal to the original equilibrium point.\n    Option C is incorrect. A decline in the stock market of Country A’s largest export market will lead to a\n    decrease in Country A’s net exports and aggregate demand, resulting in a deflationary (output) gap.\n    There will be excess supply in the factor market, resulting in a downward pressure on input prices. The\n    cost of production will decrease. Short run aggregate supply will increase. In the long run, the price\n    level at the new equilibrium point will be lower than that at the original equilibrium point.\n    Option D is incorrect. A lower transportation cost for raw materials will cause short run aggregate\n    supply to increase and result in an inflationary (output) gap. There will be excess demand in the factor\n    market, resulting in an upward pressure on input prices. The cost of production will increase. Short run\n    aggregate supply will decrease. In the long run, the new equilibrium point will be equal to the original\n    equilibrium point.",
