      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/31/q-p1-35.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/31/a-p1-35.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The following table shows the balance sheet of the banking system of a country. The monetary base of the country\n      is $700. The banks hold excess reserves of $100.\n\n                                     Assets ($)                            Liabilities ($)\n                       Reserves                         500   Deposits                        2,000\n                       Loans                          1,500\n\n      Suppose the public deposit $100 cash into the banking system. If the banks lend out all of their excess reserves,\n      which of the following statements is/are CORRECT?\n\n            (1)   The deposits will increase by $1,000.\n            (2)   The loans will increase by $300.\n            (3)   The money supply will be $3,100.\n\n            A.    (1) only\n            B.    (1) and (3) only\n            C.    (2) and (3) only\n            D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    Required reserve ratio = [($500 ‒ $100) / $2,000] × 100% = 20%\n    Monetary base = Cash in public circulation + Bank reserves\n    Cash in public circulation = $700 ‒ $500 = $200\n    (1) is correct. New deposits = ($500 + $100) × (1/20%) = $3,000\n    Change in deposits = $3,000 ‒ $2,000 = $1,000\n    (3) is correct. Money supply = Cash in public circulation + Bank deposits\n    New money supply = ($200 ‒ $100) + $3,000 = $3,100\n    (2) is incorrect. New loans = $3,000 ‒ $600 = $2,400\n    Change in loans = $2,400 ‒ $1,500 = $900",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/31/ae-p1-35.jpg",
      "originalQuestionImageEng": "originals/31/qe-p1-35.jpg",
