      "questionTextEng": "In times of inflation, suppose the nominal interest rate of a fixed-interest bond is higher than the expected\n      inflation rate. If the expected inflation rate is higher than the actual inflation rate,\n\n            A.     the real rate of return on holding cash will be greater than the nominal rate of return on holding cash.\n            B.     the nominal rate of return on holding cash will be greater than zero.\n            C.     the expected real interest rate will be lower than the actual real interest rate.\n            D.     the cost of holding cash will decrease.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    Expected real interest rate = Nominal interest rate – Expected inflation rate\n    Actual real interest rate = Nominal interest rate – Actual inflation rate\n    If the expected inflation rate is higher than the actual inflation rate, the expected real interest rate is\n    lower than the actual real interest rate.\n    Option A is incorrect.\n    Real rate of return on holding cash = Nominal rate of return on holding cash  Actual inflation rate\n    Real rate of return on holding cash = 0  Actual inflation rate\n    Since the actual inflation rate is greater than zero, the real rate of return on holding cash is smaller than\n    the nominal rate of return on holding cash.\n    Option B is incorrect.\n    Cash does not bear interest. Therefore, cash does not generate any nominal return for the holder. The\n    nominal rate of return on holding cash is zero.\n    Option D is incorrect. Cost of holding cash = Nominal interest rate. As the nominal interest rate remains\n    unchanged, the cost of holding cash will remain unchanged.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/31/ae-p1-38.jpg",
      "originalQuestionImageEng": "originals/31/qe-p1-38.jpg",
