      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/31/a-p1-39.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Suppose the unemployment rate of an economy is 5%. Which of the following would lower the unemployment\n      rate?\n\n            (1)    Some graduates who studied abroad return to the economy to find a job and 3% of them fail to find\n                   one.\n            (2)    Some workers who have been unemployed for a long time emigrate to other countries.\n            (3)    Some retirees return to work.\n\n            A.     (1) and (2) only\n            B.     (1) and (3) only\n            C.     (2) and (3) only\n            D.     (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    Unemployment rate = (Unemployed population / Labour force) × 100%\n    (1) is correct. Since 3% of this group of graduates are unsuccessful in finding a job, the percentage\n    increase in the unemployed population is smaller than the percentage increase in the labour force and\n    thus the unemployment rate would decrease.\n    (2) is correct. Since the decrease in the number of the unemployed is the same as the decrease in the\n    labour force, the percentage decrease in the unemployed population is greater than the percentage\n    decrease in the labour force, and the unemployment rate would decrease.\n    (3) is correct. Retired people originally are not included in the unemployed population and the labour\n    force. When they return to the job market, the labour force would increase while the unemployed\n    population would remain unchanged. The unemployment rate would decrease.",
