      "graphType": "-",
      "tableType": "-",
      "multipleSelectionType": "四項陳述組合",
      "calculationType": "-",
      "source": "模擬試卷三十二 卷一",
      "reviewedByAI": "Y",
      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/32/q-p1-16.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/32/a-p1-16.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "When the government provides a per-unit subsidy of $6 to the producers of good X, its price may __________ if\n      the elasticity of demand for good X is __________ its elasticity of supply.\n\n            (1)   decrease by $2 … higher than\n            (2)   decrease by $4 … higher than\n            (3)   decrease by $4 … lower than\n            (4)   decrease by $3 … equal to\n\n            A.    (2) only\n            B.    (1) and (3) only\n            C.    (2) and (4) only\n            D.    (1), (3) and (4) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    If the demand for good X is more elastic than its supply, the consumers’ share of the subsidy will be\n    smaller than the producers’ share of the subsidy.\n    (1) is correct. If the price of good X decreases by $2, the consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($2)\n    will be smaller than the producers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($4).\n    (2) is incorrect. If the price of good X falls by $4, the consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($4) will\n    be greater than the producers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($2).\n    If the demand for good X is less elastic than its supply, the consumers’ share of the subsidy will be\n    greater than the producers’ share of the subsidy.\n    (3) is correct. If the price of good X decreases by $4, the consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($4)\n    will be greater than the producers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($2).\n    If the demand for good X is as elastic as its supply, the consumers’ share of the subsidy will be equal to\n    the producers’ share of the subsidy.\n    (4) is correct. If the price of good X decreases by $3, the consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($3)\n    will equal the producers’ share of the subsidy per unit ($3).",
