      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/32/q-p1-19.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/32/a-p1-19.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following statements about a perfectly competitive market is CORRECT?\n\n            A.    Different sellers sell similar but distinguishable products.\n            B.    The market price will not change.\n            C.    Sellers have perfect market information while buyers do not have perfect market information.\n            D.    The marginal revenue of a firm equals to the market price.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    In a perfectly competitive market, all buyers and sellers make consumption and production decisions\n    based on a given price. The firm can sell as much as it wants at the prevailing market price and it has no\n    influence on the market price. Therefore, the revenue from selling an additional unit of output, i.e.\n    marginal revenue, is equal to the price.\n    Option A is incorrect. In a perfectly competitive market, sellers sell homogeneous products.\n    Option B is incorrect. In a perfectly competitive market, individual firms cannot influence the market\n    price. However, the market price will still change due to changes in market demand and/or market\n    supply.\n    Option C is incorrect. In a perfectly competitive market, both buyers and sellers have perfect market\n    information.",
