      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/32/a-p1-20.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "An increase in the supply of wool\n\n            A.    will lead to an increase in the marginal benefit of the last unit of wool.\n            B.    will improve economic efficiency.\n            C.    can be caused by an increase in the price of wool.\n            D.    can be caused by an increase in demand for lamb.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    Lamb and wool are in joint supply. When the demand for lamb increases, the price of lamb will increase,\n    leading to an increase in the supply of wool.\n    Option A is incorrect. Consumers will buy wool until the price equals the marginal benefit. When the\n    supply of wool increases, its price will decrease and the marginal benefit of the last unit of wool will\n    decrease.\n    Option B is incorrect. An increase in the supply of wool will increase the total social surplus, but this\n    does not mean that economic efficiency has improved. Before and after the change in supply, at the\n    equilibrium quantity, the marginal benefit is equal to the marginal cost and the total social surplus has\n    reached the maximum, i.e. efficiency has been achieved. Therefore, an increase in supply will not\n    improve efficiency.\n\n    Option C is incorrect. An increase in the price of wool leads to an increase in the quantity supplied of\n    wool, not an increase in the supply.",
