      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows the Gini coefficient of Country A.\n\n                                                                            2021              2022\n                  Gini coefficients based on post-tax post-social\n                                                                            0.474             0.453\n                  transfer monthly household income\n\n      Which of the following best explains the change in Country A from 2021 to 2022?\n\n           A.    The government has reduced the transfer payments to low-income groups.\n           B.    The government has tightened the eligibility criteria for applying for subsidised housing.\n           C.    The government has raised the tobacco tax.\n           D.    The government has raised the progressivity of salaries tax.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    When the progressiveness of salaries tax increases, the average tax rate paid by high-income groups will\n    increase by a larger amount than that paid by low-income groups. This can narrow the income gap and\n    reduce the Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social transfer monthly household income.\n    Option A is incorrect. The post-tax post-social transfer household income of the recipients will decrease.\n    As the recipients are mainly low-income people, the income gap between the rich and the poor will\n    widen. The Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social transfer monthly household income will\n    increase.\n    Option B is incorrect. When the government tightens the eligibility criteria for applying for subsidised\n    housing, fewer people are eligible to apply for subsidised housing, and the number of low-income\n    people receiving subsidised housing will decrease. The income gap between the rich and the poor will\n    widen. The Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social transfer monthly household income will\n    increase.\n    Option C is incorrect. Tobacco tax is a regressive tax. As the same amount of tax increase takes up a\n    higher proportion of the income of the low-income group than that of the high-income group, the\n    income gap between the rich and the poor will widen. The Gini coefficient based on post-tax post-social\n    transfer monthly household income will increase.",
