      "questionTextEng": "Suppose a country has a deflationary (output) gap. Which of the following would narrow the deflationary (output)\n      gap and the income gap at the same time?\n\n             A.       to increase transfer payments\n             B.       to raise the discount rate\n             C.       to raise the sales tax rate\n             D.       to lower the profits tax rate",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    Increasing transfer payments can increase people’s disposable income. This will cause private\n    consumption expenditure to increase and aggregate demand to increase. The deflationary (output) gap\n    will be narrowed. Since most of the recipients of transfer payments are low-income people, the income\n    gap will be narrowed.\n    Option B is incorrect. Raising the discount rate will reduce the money supply. The interest rate will\n    increase and consumption/investment will decrease. Aggregate demand will decrease and the\n    deflationary (output) gap will widen.\n    Option C is incorrect. Sales tax is a regressive tax. Raising the sales tax rate will widen the income gap.\n    A higher sales tax rate will reduce the short run aggregate supply and widen the deflationary (output)\n    gap.\n    Option D is incorrect. Reducing the profits tax rate will increase investment expenditure. Aggregate\n    demand will increase and the deflationary (output) gap will be narrowed. However, as most people\n    paying the profits tax are high-income people, reducing the profits tax rate will widen the income gap.",
