      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/33/q-p1-09.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/33/a-p1-09.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following would raise both the occupational mobility of labour and average labour productivity of\n      an economy?\n\n            A.    The government provides more vocational training courses.\n            B.    The government increases the quota of its skilled immigration scheme.\n            C.    The government sets up a website to provide information on job vacancies.\n            D.    More professional groups tighten their licensing requirements.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n     The provision of more vocational training courses can improve people’s skills, thereby increasing\n     average labour productivity and increasing people’s ability and willingness to change from one\n     occupation to another.\n     Option B is incorrect. The increase in the quota of the skilled immigration scheme will increase average\n     labour productivity, but occupational mobility of labour will not be affected.\n     Option C is incorrect. Setting up a website to provide information on job vacancies will increase\n     occupational mobility of labour, but average labour productivity will not be affected.\n     Option D is incorrect. More professional groups tightening their licensing requirements will make it\n     more difficult for people to change occupation, which will reduce occupational mobility of labour, while\n    the effect on average labour productivity is uncertain.",
