      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/33/a-p1-14.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows the cost-output relationship of a price taker. The fixed cost of the firm is $2.\n\n                                        Output (units)              Average cost ($)\n                                             1                            10\n                                             2                            11\n                                             3                            12\n                                             4                            13\n                                             5                            14\n\n      If the average cost per unit of output falls by $2 and the market price is $12,\n\n            A.    diseconomies of scale will emerge.\n            B.    the profit-maximising output will increase from 3 units to 5 units.\n            C.    the maximum profit of the firm will be $6.\n            D.    the minimum total cost of the firm will be $30.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    When the average cost per unit of output falls by $2:\n        Output       Average      Total cost     Marginal\n        (units)      cost ($)        ($)         cost ($)\n           1             8             8             6\n           2             9            18            10\n           3            10            30            12\n           4            11            44            14\n           5            12            60            16\n\n    The market price is $12 and the profit-maximising output is 3 units (MC = MR = Price).\n    Maximum profit = $12 × 3 ‒ $30 = $6\n    Option A is incorrect. There is a fixed cost, which shows that the firm produces in the short run, and\n    diseconomies of scale do not apply to short-run production.\n    Option B is incorrect. The profit-maximising output will increase from 2 units to 3 units.\n    Option D is incorrect. If the firm chooses to produce 1 unit, the total cost ($8) will be lower than $30.",
