      "inlineDiagrams": "diagrams/MT33-P1-29.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following post-tax post-social transfer Lorenz curves of Country A.\n\n                                100\n\n                                 80\n\n                  Cumulative\n                  percentage     60\n                  of post-tax\n                  post-social\n                                 40             Lorenz curve\n                  transfer\n                                                in 2023\n                  income\n\n                                 20\n                                                                    Lorenz curve\n                                                                    in 2020\n\n                                 0         20        40        60       80      100\n                                        Cumulative percentage of households\n\n      With reference to the above information, which of the following can explain the change from 2020 to 2023?\n\n            A.     The government reduced the transfer payments to the poor between 2020 and 2023.\n            B.     Between 2020 and 2023, the tax system changed from regressive to progressive.\n            C.     The government reduced the basic allowance of the salaries tax between 2020 and 2023.\n            D.     Between 2020 and 2023, the income of low-income households decreased while the income of high-\n                   income households increased.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    Country A’s Lorenz curve in 2023 is closer to the line of perfect income equality than the Lorenz curve\n    in 2020, showing that income inequality has improved in 2023. A progressive tax system can help\n    improve income inequality.\n    Option A is incorrect. Reducing the transfer payments to the poor will worsen income inequality.\n    Option C is incorrect. After reducing the basic allowance of the salaries tax, those low-income people\n    who did not have to pay tax before may have to pay tax. The post-tax post-social transfer income of\n    low-income people will decrease, worsening income inequality.\n    Option D is incorrect. The Lorenz curve shows the income distribution of the economy and does not\n    reflect the income of households.",
