      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/34/a-p1-14.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The government decides to provide a per-unit subsidy of $6 to battery manufacturers. Suppose the equilibrium\n      price of batteries before the provision of the subsidy is $30. If the elasticity of demand for batteries is higher than\n      the elasticity of supply, the equilibrium price after the provision of the subsidy can be __________.\n\n            (1)   24\n            (2)   27\n            (3)   29\n            (4)   30\n\n            A.    (1) only\n            B.    (3) only\n            C.    (1) and (2) only\n            D.    (3) and (4) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    (3) is correct. If the demand for batteries is more elastic than the supply, producers’ share of the subsidy\n    will be larger than consumers’ share of the subsidy. Consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit of batteries\n    will be smaller than $3. Therefore, the equilibrium price after the provision of the per-unit subsidy can\n    be $29 (Consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit of batteries = $1).\n    (4) is correct. If the demand for batteries is perfectly elastic or the supply of batteries is perfectly\n    inelastic (demand is more elastic than supply), after the provision of the per-unit subsidy, the\n    equilibrium price will remain unchanged, i.e. $30.\n    (1) and (2) are incorrect. As mentioned above, consumers’ share of the subsidy per unit of batteries will\n    be smaller than $3, therefore, the new equilibrium price cannot be $24 or $27.",
