      "graphType": "-",
      "tableType": "-",
      "multipleSelectionType": "三項陳述組合",
      "calculationType": "-",
      "source": "模擬試卷三十四 卷一",
      "reviewedByAI": "Y",
      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/34/q-p1-16.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/34/a-p1-16.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following demand-supply schedule of good X.\n\n        Unit price ($)                    10           11            12        13           14           15\n        Quantity demanded (units)         45           40            35        30           25           20\n        Quantity supplied (units)         25           30            35        40           45           50\n\n      If the government imposes a per-unit tax of $2 on good X,\n\n           (1)   within the range of the original and new market prices, the demand for good X is inelastic.\n           (2)   producers’ total revenue net of tax will decrease.\n           (3)   consumers and producers bear the same tax burden.\n\n           A.    (1) and (2) only\n           B.    (1) and (3) only\n           C.    (2) and (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n      Unit price ($)                     10            11        12       13           14          15\n      Quantity demanded (units)          45            40        35       30           25          20\n      Quantity supplied (units)          25            30        35       40           45          50\n      Quantity supplied after tax\n                                           -           -         25       30           35          40\n      (units)\n    Before tax, the equilibrium price of good X is $12 and the quantity transacted is 35 units.\n    After tax, the equilibrium price of good X is $13 and the quantity transacted is 30 units.\n    (2) is correct. Before tax, producers’ total revenue is $420. After tax, producers’ total revenue net of tax\n    is $330 (= ($13 ‒ $2) × 30).\n    (3) is correct. Consumers’ tax burden = ($13 – $12) × 30 units = $30\n    Producers’ tax burden = [$12 – ($13 – $2)] × 30 units = $30\n    Consumers and producers bear the same tax burden.\n    (1) is incorrect. Before tax, consumers’ total expenditure on good X is $420. After tax, the price of\n    good X increases and consumers’ total expenditure on good X decreases to $390. This indicates that the\n    percentage increase in price is smaller than the percentage decrease in quantity demanded, and the\n    demand for good X is elastic.",
