      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/34/a-p1-26.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The following table shows the gross domestic product (GDP) data of an economy for a certain year.\n\n                                 GDP components                                   $ million\n                  Changes in inventories                                            −30\n                  Net domestic fixed capital formation                              500\n                  Capital consumption                                                10\n                  Net exports                                                       100\n                  Net factor income from abroad                                     150\n                  Indirect taxes                                                     30\n                  Subsidies                                                          50\n                  Direct taxes                                                       70\n\n      Which of the following statements about the economy are CORRECT?\n\n            (1)   The gross domestic fixed capital formation is larger than the net domestic fixed capital formation.\n            (2)   The gross national income (GNI) is larger than the GDP.\n            (3)   The GDP at market prices is larger than the GDP at factor cost.\n\n            A.    (1) and (2) only\n            B.    (1) and (3) only\n            C.    (2) and (3) only\n            D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    (1) is correct.\n    Gross domestic fixed capital formation = Net domestic fixed capital formation + Depreciation\n    As depreciation (i.e. capital consumption) is 10 million, the gross domestic fixed capital formation is\n    larger than the net domestic fixed capital formation.\n    (2) is correct.\n    GNI = GDP + Net factor income from abroad\n    As net factor income from abroad is positive, GNI is greater than GDP.\n    (3) is incorrect. GDP at market prices = GDP at factor cost + Indirect taxes – Subsidies\n    As indirect taxes are smaller than subsidies, GDP at market prices is smaller than GDP at factor cost.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/34/ae-p1-26.jpg",
