      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following diagram.\n\n           Growth rate (%)\n\n                                                                        General price level\n\n                                                                        Money supply\n\n                   0\n                                                                                                Year\n\n      According to the quantity theory of money,\n\n            A.    the growth rate of real output is decreasing.\n            B.    the inflation rate is decreasing.\n            C.    the velocity of circulation of money is increasing.\n            D.    the money supply remains unchanged.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    Quantity theory of money: MV = PY, where M = money supply, V = velocity of circulation of money, P\n    = price level and Y = real output, assuming V is constant.\n    % change in M ≈ % change in nominal GDP ≈ % change in P + % change in Y\n    When the growth rate of M is positive and constant, and the growth rate of P changes from a growth rate\n    lower than that of M to a growth rate higher than that of M, the percentage change in Y changes from\n    positive to negative. The growth rate of real output is decreasing.\n    Option B is incorrect. The inflation rate is equal to the growth rate of the general price level. The\n    diagram shows that the inflation rate is increasing.\n    Option C is incorrect. The quantity theory of money assumes that the velocity of circulation of money is\n    a constant.\n    Option D is incorrect. The growth rate of the money supply is positive, meaning that the money supply\n    is increasing.",
