      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following would offset the effect of a contractionary monetary policy on aggregate output?\n\n           A.    abolishing the general sales tax\n           B.    raising the profits tax rate\n           C.    increasing government budget surplus\n           D.    selling government bonds to the public",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    When the government implements a contractionary monetary policy, aggregate demand will decrease\n    and aggregate output will decrease.\n    The abolition of the general sales tax will reduce the production cost, the short run aggregate supply will\n    increase. Aggregate output will increase, which can offset the effect of a contractionary monetary policy\n    on aggregate output.\n    Option B is incorrect. Raising the profits tax rate will reduce firms’ investment, investment expenditure\n    will decrease. Aggregate demand will decrease. Aggregate output will decrease further.\n    Option C is incorrect. When the government budget surplus increases, government tax revenue is\n    greater than government expenditure. The effect on aggregate demand is contractionary. Aggregate\n    output will decrease further.\n    Option D is incorrect. When the central bank sells government bonds to the public, the money supply\n    will decrease. The interest rate will increase. Consumption/investment will decrease. Aggregate demand\n    will decrease. Aggregate output will decrease further.",
