      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/34/a-p1-41.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Suppose the liquidity of interest-bearing assets in an economy decreases. Which of the following policies can\n      stabilise the output level and the interest rate in the economy at the same time in the short run?\n\n           A.    The central bank raises the discount rate.\n           B.    The central bank lowers the required reserve ratio.\n           C.    The government raises the salaries tax rate.\n           D.    The government changes the payment of salaries of civil servants from bi-weekly to monthly.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    When the liquidity of interest-bearing assets decreases, people will hold less of these interest-bearing\n    assets and hold more money. Asset demand for money will increase and the interest rate will increase.\n    Consumption and investment expenditure will decrease. Aggregate demand will decrease and the output\n    level will decrease.\n    Option B is correct. Lowering the required reserve ratio by the central bank will increase the money\n    supply. The interest rate will decrease, which will stimulate consumption and investment expenditure.\n    Aggregate demand will increase and the output level will increase, thus stabilising the output level and\n    the interest rate in the economy.\n    Option A is incorrect. When the central bank raises the discount rate, the money supply will decrease\n    and the interest rate will increase further.\n    Option C is incorrect. When the government raises the salaries tax rate, people’s disposable income will\n    decrease and private consumption expenditure will decrease. Aggregate demand will decrease and the\n    output level will decrease further.\n    Option D is incorrect. When the government increases the time interval between income payments for\n    civil servants, people will increase the amount of money they hold for transactions. Transaction demand\n    for money will increase and the interest rate will increase further.",
