      "questionTextEng": "Mr. Chan lives in a residential unit that he owns. He is considering whether to sell the residential unit or renovate\n     it before continuing to live in it. He will need to rent a residential unit to live in during the renovation period. In\n     which of the following situations will his cost of continuing to live in his residential unit increase?\n\n           A.    The market price of Mr. Chan’s residential unit decreases.\n           B.    The renovation work is delayed.\n           C.    The market rent of other residential units decreases.\n           D.    The quality of the renovation work turns out to be better than expected.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     Mr. Chan’s cost of continuing to live in the residential unit is the value of selling the residential unit and\n     the monetary cost of continuing to live in the residential unit (i.e. the renovation cost and the rent paid to\n     rent a residential unit during the renovation period).\n     Option B is correct. If the renovation work is delayed, Mr. Chan will have to rent another residential\n     unit to live in for a longer period, which will increase his monetary cost of continuing to live in the\n     residential unit.\n     Option A is incorrect. Mr. Chan has to give up the income that could have been obtained from selling\n     the residential unit. If the market price of the residential unit decreases, his cost of continuing to live in\n     the residential unit will decrease.\n     Option C is incorrect. If the market rent of other residential units decreases, the monetary cost of\n     continuing to live in the residential unit will decrease.\n     Option D is incorrect. If the quality of the renovation work turns out to be better than expected, the\n     value of continuing to live in the residential unit will increase, but the highest-valued option forgone\n     will not be affected.",
