      "questionTextEng": "In recent years, public dental services in Hong Kong have been facing a shortage of dentists, which has\n                                                           affected the services of government dental clinics. The situation has raised public concerns.\n\n                                                          Source A: News extract about the registration arrangements for public dental services\n                                                           Public dental services in Hong Kong primarily serve civil servants/retired civil servants and their\n                                                           eligible dependents, offering very limited services to the general public in urgent need. As of\n                                                           February 2024, there are 43 government dental clinics in Hong Kong, 11 of which allocate specific\n                                                           sessions each week, commonly referred to as ‘general public (GP) sessions (牙科街症)’, to provide\n                                                           free emergency dental treatment services to members of the public in need.\n\n                                                           Starting from 11 June 2024, the preliminary registration time for the GP sessions at 9 government\n\n                                                           dental clinics has been adjusted from midnight on the day of service to 8 pm the day before. Due to\n                                                           the limited quota, each person can only register once, and the spots are allocated on a first-come,\n                                                           first-served basis. Some citizens have stated that although the registration time has changed, people\n                                                           still must queue up outside the clinics. Those who are unable to register will need to leave the clinic\n                                                           and will not be eligible for treatment at the GP session the following day.\n\n                                                          Source B: News extract about the suspension of Civil Servants Dental Service\n                                                           The Department of Health has been facing a shortage of dentists. According to the government\n                                                           data, the vacancy rate for dentists in the Department of Health reached 29.7% (as of the beginning\n                                                           of July 2024). Due to the shortage of dentists, services at approximately a quarter of the dental\n                                                           clinics providing Civil Servants Dental Service have been suspended. The manpower at school\n                                                           dental clinics and dental clinics in public hospitals has also been affected.\n\n                                                          Source C: Some views on Hong Kong’s dental labour market\n                                                                                                   The Faculty of Dentistry at the University of Hong Kong is currently\n                                                                                                   the only institution that trains dentists in Hong Kong. I think the\n                                                                                                   government should establish a new dental school to increase the\n                                                                                                   number of local dentists.\n                                                                 A university professor\n                                                                                                   Even if the government raises the wage rate of public dentists, it is\n                                                                                                   still difficult to alleviate the shortage of dentists faced by the\n                                                                                                   government dental clinics.\n\n                                                                     A commentator\n                                                                                                   Adjusting the remuneration of public dentists can attract dental\n                                                                                                   graduates to join public institutions instead of private institutions.\n\n                                                                    A dental graduate\n\n                                                        (a) Refer to Source A. Explain whether the cost for citizens of using free emergency dental treatment services\n                                                            is necessarily the same.                                                                       (3 marks)\n\n                                                        (b) Refer to Sources B and C. With the aid of a supply-demand diagram, explain how the university\n                                                            professor’s suggestion in Source C could improve the labour market in public dental services. (7 marks)\n\n                                                        (c) Refer to Source C. Explain whether the statement of the dental graduate is a positive statement or a\n                                                            normative statement.                                                                       (2 marks)\n\n                                                        (d) Justify the statement of the commentator in Source C using the concept of elasticity of supply/demand.\n                                                                                                                                                             (3 marks)\n\n                                                        For part (e), candidates are required to present their answer in essay form. Criteria for marking will include use\n                                                        of sources and economic theories, relevant content, logical presentation and clarity of expression.\n\n                                                        (e) Suppose two proposals are suggested to address the problem of manpower shortage in Hong Kong’s public\n                                                            dental services.\n\n                                                               Proposal I: To distribute ‘dental care vouchers’ to subsidise citizens’ use of private dental services\n                                                               Proposal II: To hire more non-locally trained dentists to provide public dental services\n\n                                                             With reference to the above sources and your own knowledge in economics, discuss which proposals,\n                                                             proposal I or II, each of the following groups of stakeholders would prefer:\n\n                                                             - politicians who are concerned about whether low-income individuals have timely access to dental\n                                                             services\n                                                             - owners of private dental clinics\n\n                                                             (Hint: You may discuss the reasons why a group of stakeholders would prefer a certain proposal OR why\n                                                             that group would not prefer the other proposal.)\n                                                                                                                                                          (7 marks)",
