      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/36/q-p1-13.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/36/a-p1-13.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following market supply schedule for apples.\n\n       Unit price ($)                    4            5            6         7           8           9\n       Quantity supplied (units)         4            6            8         10          12          14\n\n     Suppose the original equilibrium price is $7. If the government provides a per-unit subsidy of $3 to the producers\n     of apples, the quantity transacted of apples will increase by 2 units. Which of the following statements is\n     CORRECT?\n\n           A.    The market price will decrease by $3.\n           B.    The total subsidy granted by the government is $24.\n           C.    The demand for apples is more elastic than the supply of them.\n           D.    The producers’ total revenue including the subsidy is $96.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    The original equilibrium price is $7 and the equilibrium quantity is 10 units. After the provision of the\n    per-unit subsidy, the new equilibrium quantity is 12 units and the new equilibrium price is $5.\n      Unit price ($)                           4          5           6          7          8           9\n      Original quantity supplied (units)       4          6           8         10         12          14\n      New quantity supplied (units)           10          12         14          -          -           -\n    Option D is correct. The producers’ total revenue including the subsidy is $96 (= $5 × 12 + $3 × 12).\n    Option A is incorrect. The new equilibrium price is $5, a decrease of $2.\n    Option B is incorrect. The total subsidy granted by the government is $36 (= $3 × 12).\n    Option C is incorrect.\n    Consumers’ share of the subsidy = $24 (= $2 × 12)\n    Producers’ share of the subsidy = $12 [= [($5 + $3) – $7] × 12]\n    The consumers’ share of the subsidy is larger than the producers’ share of the subsidy, implying that the\n    supply is more elastic than the demand.",
