      "questionTextEng": "To calculate Hong Kong’s gross national income (GNI), which of the following should be added to Hong Kong’s\n    gross domestic product (GDP)?\n\n           A.    the dividends received by a Hong Kong resident from shares in South Korea’s Samsung Group\n           B.    the salary earned by a Hong Kong chef working at a hotel in Japan for two years\n           C.    the salary earned by a Hong Kong resident working at the Hong Kong branch of a German company\n           D.    the rent received by a Japanese resident from property held in Hong Kong",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    GNI = GDP + Factor income earned by residents outside the economic territory − Factor income earned\n    by non-residents within the economic territory\n    Option A is correct. This is factor income earned by a resident outside the economic territory.\n    Options B is incorrect. Since the chef has been working in Japan for more than a year, he is no longer\n    considered a resident of Hong Kong. Therefore, the income he earned would not be classified as factor\n    income earned by a resident outside the economic territory.\n    Option C is incorrect. It is income earned by a resident within the economic territory.\n    Option D is incorrect. This is factor income earned by a non-resident within the economic territory.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/36/ae-p1-26.jpg",
