      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following can narrow the deflationary (output) gap and help equalise opportunities in an economy at\n    the same time?\n\n           (1)   distributing the same amount of cash to all households\n           (2)   arranging for more civil servants to study abroad\n           (3)   providing subsidies on medicine for patients with chronic diseases\n\n           A.    (1) only\n           B.    (2) only\n           C.    (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    (3) is correct. The provision of subsidies on medicine for patients with chronic diseases will make it\n    easier for them to obtain the medicines they need. They are less likely to be deprived of necessary\n    medications due to financial constraints. This is likely to increase their future productivity and income\n    and help equalise opportunities. Providing subsidies on medicine can also increase the disposable\n    income of the recipients. Private consumption expenditure will increase and aggregate demand will\n    increase, narrowing the deflationary (output) gap.\n    (1) is incorrect. Compared to the rich, the poor do not get more resources to improve their productivity\n    or increase their future income, so distributing the same amount of cash to all households will not help\n    equalise opportunities.\n    (2) is incorrect. This ensures that the civil servants do not lose the opportunity for further studies due to\n    financial constraints, thereby helping equalise opportunities. However, arranging for more civil servants\n    to study abroad will reduce the labour force. The short run aggregate supply will decrease, thus\n    widening the deflationary (output) gap.",
