      "inlineDiagrams": "diagrams/MT37-P1-20.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following supply-demand diagram for good X. Initially, the market for good X is at equilibrium.\n\n                            Unit price\n                                                                S\n\n                              P\n\n                                                                     D\n                               0                                           Quantity\n                                                Q\n\n      Which of the following situations will reduce producer surplus and create a deadweight loss in the market of\n      good X at the same time?\n\n            (1)   the imposition of a per-unit sales tax on good X\n            (2)   the imposition of an effective quota on good X\n            (3)   the imposition of an effective price ceiling on good X\n            (4)   a decrease in the demand for good X\n\n            A.    (1) and (3) only\n            B.    (2) and (4) only\n            C.    (1), (2) and (3) only\n            D.    (2), (3) and (4) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    (1) is correct. After a per-unit sales tax is imposed, the quantity transacted will decrease and the price\n     received by producers will also decrease. Producer surplus will decrease. As the quantity of output is\n     below the efficient output level, a deadweight loss is created in the market.\n     (3) is correct. After an effective price ceiling is imposed, both the price and quantity transacted will\n     decrease. Producer surplus will decrease. As the quantity of output is below the efficient output level, a\n     deadweight loss is created in the market.\n     (2) is incorrect. After an effective quota is imposed, the price will increase and the quantity transacted\n     will decrease. Producer surplus may increase, decrease or remain unchanged, depending on the price\n     elasticity of demand.\n     (4) is incorrect. A decrease in demand will cause the price and quantity transacted to decrease, and\n     producer surplus will decrease. However, as marginal benefit equals marginal cost at the quantity\n    transacted, there will be no deadweight loss.",
