      "questionTextEng": "A shopping mall plans to hold outdoor concerts on its rooftop on weekend nights. However, nearby residents\n      oppose the plan, believing that the concerts would cause noise pollution. Which of the following statements is\n      CORRECT?\n\n            A.    The noise problem can be eliminated if the shopping mall gives compensation to the affected\n                  residents.\n            B.    The divergence between private and social costs can be narrowed if the shopping mall gives\n                  compensation to the affected residents.\n            C.    There will be no divergence between private and social costs if the shopping mall has the right to hold\n                  outdoor concerts on its rooftop.\n            D.    If the government imposes a fine on the shopping mall for excessive music noise, this will help reduce\n                  the problem of under-production of music.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     If the shopping mall gives compensation to the affected residents, the external cost can be internalised,\n     reducing the divergence between private and social costs.\n     Option A is incorrect. If the shopping mall gives compensation to the affected residents, this money will\n     increase the marginal private cost of holding outdoor concerts. The quantity of noise created by the\n     shopping mall will be reduced to a level closer to the socially optimal quantity. However, noise\n     pollution may still exist.\n     Option C is incorrect. Even if the shopping mall has the right to hold outdoor concerts on its rooftop, the\n     marginal social cost is still higher than the marginal private cost, so the divergence between private and\n     social costs still exists.\n     Option D is incorrect. As marginal social cost is higher than marginal social benefit at the equilibrium\n     quantity, from the viewpoint of society, there is over-production of music, instead of under-production.",
