      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/37/a-p1-43.jpg",
      "inlineDiagrams": "diagrams/MT37-P1-43.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The diagram below shows the imposition of a quota on an imported good in a small open economy.\n\n                        Price\n\n                                                               S without quota\n\n                                                                     S with quota\n\n                        P1\n                        Pw                                             World price\n\n                                                                         D\n                                                                                 Quantity\n                       0            Q1   Q2            Q3    Q4\n    If the world price of the good decreases,\n\n           A.    the domestic price of the good will be lower than P1.\n           B.    the import volume of the good will be smaller than (Q 3 − Q2).\n           C.    the revenue of domestic producers of the good will remain unchanged.\n           D.    the total import value of the good will be P1 × (Q3 − Q2).",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    When the world price decreases, the import volume of the good will remain unchanged and equal to the\n    quota limit. The domestic price of the good will remain at P1. The domestic production will remain\n    unchanged at Q2 and the revenue of domestic producers will remain unchanged.\n    Option A is incorrect. The domestic price of the good will remain unchanged at P1.\n    Option B is incorrect. The import volume will remain unchanged at the quota limit, i.e. (Q3 − Q2).\n    Option D is incorrect. The original total import value is Pw × (Q3 − Q2). As the world price decreases\n    while the import volume remains unchanged at the quota limit, the total import value will decrease, i.e.\n    be smaller than Pw × (Q3 − Q2).",
