      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/38/a-p1-11.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The following table shows the input-output relationship of firm A and firm B.\n\n                                                                           Firm A           Firm B\n                   Number of workers                                         30               40\n                   Number of working hours per worker per day                10                6\n                   Quantity of output (units)                               3,000            3,600\n\n      Which of the following is a possible reason for the difference in labour productivity between firm A and firm B?\n\n            A.    Firm A practises a higher degree of division of labour.\n            B.    Firm A adopts piece rate while firm B adopts time rate.\n            C.    The management of firm B is better.\n            D.    The working environment of firm B is worse than firm A.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n                                          Firm A                                    Firm B\n      Labour supply       10 man-hours  30 = 300 man-hours         6 man-hours  40 = 240 man-hours\n      Average labour      3,000 units / 300 man-hours               3,600 units / 240 man-hours\n      productivity        = 10 units/man-hour                       = 15 units/man-hour\n     According to the above table, the average labour productivity of firm B is higher than that of firm A.\n    Option B is incorrect. If firm A adopts piece rate and firm B adopts time rate, since the income of\n    workers of firm A depends on output, the labour productivity of firm A should be higher than that of\n    firm B, which is inconsistent with the data in the question.",
