      "inlineDiagrams": "diagrams/MT38-P1-29.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The diagram below shows Lorenz curves for Country A, constructed based on post-tax post-social transfer\n      household income.\n\n                                100\n\n                                 80\n\n                  Cumulative\n                  percentage     60\n                  of post-tax\n                  post-social\n                                 40              Lorenz curve\n                  transfer\n                                                 in 2023\n                  household\n                  income\n                                 20\n                                                                     Lorenz curve\n                                                                     in 2021\n\n                                 0          20        40        60       80       100\n                                         Cumulative percentage of households\n\n      Which of the following can best explain the above change in the Lorenz curve?\n\n           A.      Between 2021 and 2023, the tax system changed from progressive to regressive.\n           B.      The government raised the standard tax rate of the salaries tax between 2021 and 2023.\n           C.      The government reduced the transfer payments to the poor between 2021 and 2023.\n           D.      Between 2021 and 2023, the percentage increase in post-tax post-social transfer income of low-\n                   income households was lower than that of high-income households.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    Country A’s Lorenz curve is closer to the line of perfect income equality in 2023 than in 2021, showing\n    that income inequality has improved in 2023. Raising the standard tax rate of the salaries tax caused the\n    post-tax post-social transfer income of high-income people to decrease, while the post-tax post-social\n    transfer income of low-income people remained unchanged, improving income inequality.\n    Option A is incorrect. A regressive tax system would worsen income inequality.\n    Option C is incorrect. Reducing the transfer payments to the poor would worsen income inequality.\n    Option D is incorrect. If the percentage increase in the post-tax post-social transfer income of\n    low-income households is lower than that of high-income households, income inequality would worsen.",
