      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/39/q-p1-10.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-10.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows the cost-output relationship of a price-taking firm.\n\n                   Output (units)                  3              4               5              6\n                   Marginal cost ($)               8              9              10             11\n                   Total fixed cost ($)            2              2               2              2\n\n     Suppose the market price is $10. Which of the following statements about the firm is CORRECT?\n\n           A.    The total cost of 5 units of output is $29.\n           B.    If the market price decreases by $2, the profit of the firm will decrease by $7.\n           C.    If the marginal cost for each unit of the output increases by $2, the profit of the firm will decrease by\n                 $3.\n           D.    If total fixed cost increases to $10, the producer surplus of the firm will decrease by $5.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     The original profit-maximising output is 5 units.\n     Profit = Total revenue – Total variable cost – Total fixed cost\n     Option B is correct. If the market price decreases by $2, the profit-maximising output will drop from 5\n     units to 3 units. The original total revenue is $50 (= $10 × 5), the new total revenue is $24 (= $8 × 3),\n     and the total revenue will decrease by $26. Total variable cost will decrease by $19 (= $9 + $10). The\n     profit will decrease by $7.\n     Option A is incorrect. The question does not provide information on the marginal cost of the first 2 units\n     of output, so the total cost of 5 units of output cannot be calculated.\n     Option C is incorrect. If the marginal cost for each unit of the output increases by $2, the\n     profit-maximising output will drop from 5 units to 3 units. The original total revenue is $50 (= $10 × 5),\n     and the new total revenue is $30 (= $10 × 3), so the total revenue will decrease by $20. Suppose the\n     original total variable cost is $x (= MC1 + MC2 + $8 + $9 + $10). The new total variable cost is $x – 13\n    [= (MC1 + $2) + (MC2 + $2) + ($8 + $2)]. Total variable cost will decrease by $13. Profit will decrease\n    by $7 (= $20 – $13).\n    Option D is incorrect. Producer surplus = Total revenue – Total variable cost\n    An increase in total fixed cost will not affect the producer surplus of the firm.",
