      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/39/q-p1-15.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-15.jpg",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    (1) is correct. If the price ceiling is effective, it means that the price ceiling is lower than the equilibrium\n    price. An increase in the price of its complements will cause the demand for good X to decrease and the\n    equilibrium price of good X will decrease. If the new equilibrium price is lower than the price ceiling,\n    the price ceiling will become ineffective.\n\n    (2) is correct. If the price ceiling is effective, the quantity transacted will equal the quantity supplied.\n    When the price of a factor of production increases, the supply of good X will decrease. The quantity\n    transacted will decrease, while the price will remain unchanged. The total revenue of producers will\n    decrease.\n                     Unit price\n\n                                             S1                        Decrease in total revenue\n                                                  S\n\n                        P\n\n                                                      D\n                        0 Q1        Q0                    Quantity\n\n    (3) is incorrect. An increase in people’s preference for good X will cause demand to increase. If the\n    price floor is effective, the marginal benefit of the last unit of good X will remain unchanged.\n\n                            Unit price\n\n                                                          S\n                P = MB0 = MB1\n\n                                                              D1\n                                                          D\n                                0                                  Quantity\n                                         Q0 Q1",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/39/ae-p1-15.jpg",
