      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/39/q-p1-16.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-16.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following statements about a perfectly competitive market are CORRECT?\n\n           (1)   The market price will not change.\n           (2)   The market price is equal to the firms’ marginal revenue.\n           (3)   There is no price competition in the market.\n           (4)   There is no non-price competition in the market.\n\n           A.    (1) and (2) only\n           B.    (1) and (3) only\n           C.    (2) and (4) only\n           D.    (3) and (4) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    (2) is correct. In a perfectly competitive market, all buyers and sellers make consumption and\n    production decisions based on a given price. The firm can sell as much as it wants at the prevailing\n    market price and it has no influence on the market price. Therefore, the revenue from selling an\n    additional unit of output, i.e. marginal revenue, is equal to the price.\n    (4) is correct. In a perfectly competitive market, since all sellers sell homogeneous products at the given\n    market price and all buyers and sellers have perfect information, it is totally unnecessary for sellers to\n    engage in non-price competition.\n    (1) is incorrect. In a perfectly competitive market, although individual firms cannot influence the market\n    price, the market price will still change due to changes in market demand and/or market supply.\n    (3) is incorrect. In a perfectly competitive market, price is the only competitive criterion in determining\n    the distribution of goods. There is price competition in a perfectly competitive market.",
