      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-24.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Consider the following data for Country A in 2023 and 2024.\n\n                         5\n                        4.5                                                                           GDP at market prices\n                         4\n                        3.5\n          ($ million)\n\n                         3\n                                                                                                      GDP at factor cost\n                        2.5\n                         2\n                        1.5\n                         1                                                                            GNI at market prices\n\n                        0.5\n                         0\n                                            2023                            2024\n\n      Based on the above information, which of the following statements is CORRECT?\n\n                  A.          Country A’s net factor income from abroad decreased in 2024.\n                  B.          Country A’s net factor income from abroad was negative in both 2023 and 2024.\n                  C.          Country A’s indirect taxes were greater than subsidies in both 2023 and 2024.\n                  D.          Country A’s subsidies decreased while indirect taxes increased between 2023 and 2024.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    GDP at market prices + Net factor income from abroad = GNI\n    The part of GNI at market prices that exceeds GDP at market prices represents the value of net factor\n    income from abroad. The difference between GDP at market prices and GNI at market prices has\n    narrowed, indicating a decrease in the net factor income from abroad.\n    Option B is incorrect. In 2023 and 2024, Country A’s GDP at market prices was smaller than GNI at\n    market prices, indicating that the net factor income from abroad was positive.\n\n     Option C is incorrect.\n     GDP at market prices = GDP at factor cost + Indirect taxes – Subsidies\n     In 2023 and 2024, Country A’s GDP at market prices was smaller than GDP at factor cost, indicating\n     that subsidies were greater than indirect taxes.\n     Option D is incorrect. The part of GDP at factor cost that exceeds GDP at market prices is subsidies\n     minus indirect taxes. It can be seen from the figures that the difference decreased. This could be caused\n    by a decrease in subsidies, or an increase in indirect taxes, or both.",
