      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/39/q-p1-28.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-28.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Suppose a typhoon hits a country and disrupts the supply of raw materials. Which of the following can offset its\n       effect on the purchasing power of the country’s currency in the short run?\n\n             A.      People’s desire to save decreases.\n             B.      Banks tend not to lend out excess reserves.\n             C.      Firms expect the price level to increase.\n             D.      More people go to work overseas.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n    When a typhoon hits a country and disrupts the supply of raw materials, the short run aggregate supply\n    will decrease. The general price level will increase and the purchasing power of money will decrease.\n    Option B is correct. When banks tend not to lend out excess reserves, the deposit creation ability of the\n    banking system of the country will decrease. The money supply will decrease and the interest rate will\n    increase. Consumption/investment will decrease and aggregate demand will decrease. In the short run,\n    the general price level will decrease and the purchasing power of money will increase.\n    Option A is incorrect. When people’s desire to save decreases, private consumption expenditure will\n    increase and aggregate demand will increase. In the short run, the general price level will increase and\n    the purchasing power of money will decrease further.\n    Option C is incorrect. When firms expect the general price level to increase, they will reduce current\n    supply. Short run aggregate supply will decrease further.\n    Option D is incorrect. When more people go to work overseas, the country’s labour force will decrease\n    and the short run aggregate supply will decrease further.",
