      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/39/q-p1-34.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-34.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Refer to the following information about an economy.\n\n                      Year         Money supply ($ million)               Real output ($ million)\n                       1                    550                                    500\n                       2                    600                                    550\n\n      According to the quantity theory of money, we can conclude that\n\n            A.    the general price level decreased in year 2.\n            B.    the velocity of circulation of money increased in year 2.\n            C.    the inflation rate in year 2 was equal to zero.\n            D.    the percentage increase in nominal output was greater than the percentage increase in real output in\n                  year 2.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    Quantity theory of money: MV = PY, where M = money supply, V = velocity of circulation of money,\n     P = price level and Y = real output.\n     Assume V is constant.\n     From year 1 to year 2, the percentage increase in the money supply was smaller than the percentage\n     increase in real output, so the percentage change in the price level was negative, i.e. the general price\n     level decreased.\n\n    Option B is incorrect. According to the quantity theory of money, the velocity of circulation of money is\n    assumed to be constant.\n    Option C is incorrect. As the percentage change in the price level was negative, the inflation rate was\n    negative.\n    Option D is incorrect. According to the quantity theory of money, the percentage change in nominal\n    output (PY) will be equal to the percentage change in the money supply. As the percentage increase in\n    the money supply was smaller than the percentage increase in real output, the percentage increase in\n    nominal output was smaller than the percentage increase in real output.",
