      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/39/a-p1-37.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Suppose a deflationary (output) gap exists in an economy. Which of the following is a fiscal policy that would help\n    the economy restore to its long run equilibrium?\n\n            A.    exempting the first $5 million of an enterprise’s profits from profits tax\n            B.    having the central bank purchase government bonds from the public\n            C.    increasing the standard rate of the salaries tax\n            D.    imposing a per-unit sales tax",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    When a deflationary (output) gap exists in an economy, an increase in aggregate demand or short run\n    aggregate supply can help the economy restore to its long run equilibrium.\n    Option A is correct. This will encourage firms to increase investment. Investment expenditure will\n    increase and aggregate demand will increase.\n    Option B is incorrect. Having the central bank purchase government bonds from the public is a\n    monetary policy, not a fiscal policy.\n    Option C is incorrect. Raising the standard rate of the salaries tax will reduce people’s disposable\n    income. Private consumption expenditure will decrease and aggregate demand will decrease.\n    Option D is incorrect. Imposing a per-unit sales tax will reduce the short run aggregate supply.",
