      "questionTextEng": "When the government increases the fixed airport departure tax charged to all travellers,\n\n            A.    there will be an excess supply of flight seats.\n            B.    the average flight distance of outbound passengers will decrease.\n            C.    government’s revenue from the airport departure tax must increase.\n            D.    the proportion of passengers choosing business class seats will increase relative to economy class\n                  seats.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n     After increasing the fixed airport departure tax charged to all travellers, the price of business class seats\n     will decrease relative to economy class seats, leading to a higher proportion of passengers choosing\n     business class seats.\n     Option A is incorrect. Increasing the fixed airport departure tax charged to all travellers will raise the\n     cost of travel, the demand for flight seats may decrease. However, since we do not know whether the\n     flight market was initially in equilibrium, a decrease in demand does not necessarily mean there will be\n     an excess supply of flight seats.\n     Option B is incorrect. After increasing the fixed airport departure tax charged to all travellers, the price\n     of long-distance flights will decrease relative to short-distance flights. The proportion of passengers\n     taking long-distance flights will increase, and the average distance of flights taken by passengers will\n     rise.\n     Option C is incorrect. After increasing the fixed airport departure tax charged to all travellers, the\n     number of flights taken by people will decrease. The government’s revenue from the airport departure\n    tax may increase, decrease or remain unchanged.",
