      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/41/q-p1-14.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/41/a-p1-14.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following combinations may result in the consumers’ share of the subsidy being smaller than the\n      producers’ share of the subsidy when a per-unit subsidy is provided for a good?\n\n           (1)   elastic demand; unitarily elastic supply\n           (2)   elastic demand; perfectly inelastic supply\n           (3)   inelastic demand; inelastic supply\n           (4)   inelastic demand; perfectly elastic supply\n\n           A.    (1) and (2) only\n           B.    (3) and (4) only\n           C.    (1), (2) and (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (4) only",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n    The sharing of the subsidy between consumers and producers depends on the relative magnitude of\n    price elasticities of demand and supply. The more elastic party will have a smaller share of the subsidy.\n    When demand is more elastic than supply, consumers’ share of the subsidy will be smaller than\n    producers’ share of the subsidy.\n    (1) is correct. When demand is elastic and supply is unitarily elastic, demand must be more elastic than\n    supply.\n    (2) is correct. When demand is elastic and supply is perfectly inelastic, demand must be more elastic\n    than supply.\n    (3) is correct. When both demand and supply are inelastic, demand may be more elastic than supply.\n    (4) is incorrect. When demand is inelastic and supply is perfectly elastic, demand must be less elastic\n    than supply.",
