      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following will cause the aggregate demand curve of an economy to shift to the right?\n\n            A.    The government provides cash subsidies to applicants waiting for public housing.\n            B.    The government cuts production subsidies to firms.\n            C.    A major trading partner experiences economic recession.\n            D.    The implicit deflator of GDP has increased for several consecutive years.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    When the government provides cash subsidies to applicants waiting for public housing, the disposable\n    income of low-income households will increase. Private consumption expenditure will increase.\n    Aggregate demand will increase and the aggregate demand curve will shift to the right.\n    Option B is incorrect. When the government cuts production subsidies to firms, short run aggregate\n    supply will decrease and the short run aggregate supply curve will shift to the left.\n    Option C is incorrect. When the major trading partner experiences economic recession, Country A’s net\n    exports will fall. Aggregate demand will decrease and the aggregate demand curve will shift to the left.\n    Option D is incorrect. An increase in the general price level will cause a movement along the aggregate\n    demand curve, not a shift of the entire aggregate demand curve.",
