      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/41/a-p1-31.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "There is an inflationary (output) gap in economy A. Which of the following will narrow the inflationary (output)\n      gap?\n\n            (1)   an increase in the labour costs\n            (2)   an increase in the progressivity of salaries tax\n            (3)   an increase in production subsidies\n\n            A.    (1) and (2) only\n            B.    (1) and (3) only\n            C.    (2) and (3) only\n            D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    (1) is correct. When there is an increase in labour costs, the short run aggregate supply will decrease. In\n    the short run, aggregate output will decrease and the inflationary (output) gap will be narrowed.\n    (2) is correct. When there is an increase in the progressivity of salaries tax, some taxpayers have to pay\n    more tax, their disposable income will decrease. Private consumption expenditure will decrease and\n    aggregate demand will decrease. In the short run, aggregate output will decrease and the inflationary\n    (output) gap will be narrowed.\n    (3) is incorrect. Increasing production subsidies will reduce the cost of production. Short run aggregate\n    supply will increase. In the short run, aggregate output will increase and the inflationary (output) gap\n    will be widened.",
