      "graphType": "-",
      "tableType": "-",
      "multipleSelectionType": "三項陳述組合",
      "calculationType": "-",
      "source": "模擬試卷四十一 卷一",
      "reviewedByAI": "Y",
      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/41/q-p1-33.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/41/a-p1-33.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following would increase the aggregate output of an economy and enhance equity at the same time?\n\n            (1)   to increase the living allowance provided to grassroots children\n            (2)   to allocate more resources to improve the quality of education in public schools\n            (3)   to abolish the general sales tax\n\n            A.    (1) and (2) only\n            B.    (1) and (3) only\n            C.    (2) and (3) only\n            D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    (1) is correct. If the government increases the living allowance provided to grassroots children, their\n    disposable income will increase. Private consumption expenditure will increase and aggregate demand\n    will increase. Aggregate output will increase. At the same time, the living allowance will make it easier\n    for grassroot children to obtain learning opportunities (such as extracurricular activities) so that they are\n    not deprived of education because of financial constraints. This will improve their productivity and their\n    income in the future. This will help equalise opportunities.\n    (2) is correct. If the government allocates more resources to improve the quality of education in public\n\n    schools, government expenditure will increase. Aggregate demand will increase and aggregate output\n    will increase. At the same time, since public schools primarily serve low-income children, improving\n    the quality of education will benefit them more, which will help improve their productivity and income\n    in the future. This will help equalise opportunities.\n    (3) is correct. Abolishing the general sales tax will reduce production costs, short run aggregate supply\n    will increase and aggregate output will increase. At the same time, sales tax is a regressive tax.\n    Compared with high-income people, the sales tax accounts for a larger proportion of the income of\n    low-income people. After abolishing the general sales tax, the income of low-income people will\n    increase by a larger proportion. This will help equalise income.",
