      "questionTextEng": "In 2025, the HKSAR Government has implemented the ‘Greater Bay Area (GBA) Youth Employment Scheme -\n     Arrangements for Mainland Youth to Work in Hong Kong’. Enterprises which have participated in the scheme and\n     employed Hong Kong young persons to work in the GBA Mainland cities may apply for the same number of\n     Mainland young persons to work in Hong Kong. Mainland young persons admitted into Hong Kong for\n     employment under the Arrangements will normally be granted permission to stay in Hong Kong with a period of\n     18 months on employment condition, or the duration of the employment contract (whichever is shorter).\n\n     Based on the above information, the above arrangement will most likely lead to\n\n           A.    an increase in Hong Kong’s labour supply.\n           B.    an increase in Hong Kong’s labour productivity.\n           C.    an increase in the geographical mobility of Mainland young persons.\n           D.    an increase in the occupational mobility of Hong Kong young persons.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n     As the ability of Mainland young persons to work in Hong Kong increases, their geographical mobility\n     will increase.\n     Option A is incorrect. When companies employ young persons from Hong Kong to work in Mainland\n     cities, the number of workers in Hong Kong will decrease. Even if the companies employ the same\n     number of young persons from the Mainland to work in Hong Kong, the effect on Hong Kong’s labour\n     supply remains uncertain, depending on the number of working hours they provide.\n     Option B is incorrect. The question does not provide information on the productivity of Hong Kong\n     young persons and Mainland young persons.\n     Option D is incorrect. The scheme has no significant effect on the ability and willingness of Hong Kong\n     young persons to change from one occupation to another.",
