      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/42/q-p1-14.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/42/a-p1-14.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The demand curve for good X is downward-sloping and its supply is perfectly inelastic. In which of the following\n    cases must the equilibrium quantity of good X increase and the equilibrium price decrease?\n\n            A. The demand for good X decreases and at the same time its supply increases.\n            B. The demand for good X increases and at the same time its supply decreases.\n            C. The demand for and supply of good X increase at the same time.\n            D. The demand for and supply of good X decrease at the same time.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n    When the demand for good X is downward-sloping and its supply is perfectly inelastic, the equilibrium\n    quantity is determined solely by supply.\n    Option A is correct. When the demand for good X decreases, the equilibrium price will decrease and the\n    equilibrium quantity will remain unchanged. When the supply increases, the equilibrium price will\n    decrease and the equilibrium quantity will increase. To conclude, the equilibrium price will decrease and\n    the equilibrium quantity will increase.\n    Option B is incorrect. When the demand for good X increases, the equilibrium price will increase and\n    the equilibrium quantity will remain unchanged. When the supply decreases, the equilibrium price will\n    increase and the equilibrium quantity will decrease. To conclude, the equilibrium price will increase and\n    the equilibrium quantity will decrease.\n    Option C is incorrect. When the demand for good X increases, the equilibrium price will increase and\n    the equilibrium quantity will remain unchanged. When the supply increases, the equilibrium price will\n    decrease and the equilibrium quantity will increase. To conclude, the equilibrium price may increase,\n    decrease or remain unchanged, and the equilibrium quantity will increase.\n    Option D is incorrect. When the demand for good X decreases, the equilibrium price will decrease and\n    the equilibrium quantity will remain unchanged. When the supply decreases, the equilibrium price will\n    increase and the equilibrium quantity will decrease. To conclude, the equilibrium price may increase,\n    decrease or remain unchanged, and the equilibrium quantity will decrease.",
