      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/42/a-p1-16.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "In which of the following cases will consumers’ total expenditure on good X increase?\n\n           (1)   The price of raw materials used to produce good X increases and the demand for good X is inelastic.\n           (2)   The government increases the effective quota for good X and the demand for good X is elastic.\n           (3)   The government imposes a per-unit tax on a substitute of good X and the demand for good X is\n                 unitarily elastic.\n\n           A.    (1) and (2) only\n           B.    (1) and (3) only\n           C.    (2) and (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    (1) is correct. When the price of raw materials used to produce good X increases, the supply of good X\n    will decrease. As the demand for good X is inelastic, the percentage increase in price will be greater\n    than the percentage decrease in quantity demanded, and consumers’ total expenditure on good X will\n    increase.\n    (2) is correct. When the government increases the effective quota for good X, the price of good X will\n    decrease and the quantity transacted will increase. As the demand for good X is elastic, the percentage\n    decrease in price will be smaller than the percentage increase in quantity demanded, and consumers’\n    total expenditure on good X will increase.\n\n    (3) is correct. When the government imposes a per-unit tax on a substitute for good X, the price of the\n    substitute will increase. Demand for good X will increase, and consumers’ total expenditure on good X\n    will increase.",
