      "questionTextEng": "The diagram below shows the aggregate demand curve, short run aggregate supply curve and long run aggregate\n      supply curve for an economy. The economy initially operates at E.\n\n                        Price level\n                                                   LRAS\n                                                          SRAS\n\n                                              E\n                          P0\n\n                          P1\n\n                                                                 AD\n                          0                                              Output level\n                                              Y0     Y1\n\n      If people’s desire to save increases,\n\n            A.    the output level will be higher than Y0 in the short run.\n            B.    the price level will be higher than P0 in the short run.\n            C.    the output level will be equal to Y0 in the long run.\n            D.    the price level will be lower than P1 in the long run.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n\n               Price level\n                                        LRAS\n                                           SRAS\n\n                                   E                  SRAS1\n                 P0\n                              E1\n                 P1\n                 P2                            E2\n                                                AD1 AD\n                  0                                         Output level\n                                   Y0     Y1\n\n    When people’s desire to save increases, private consumption expenditure will decrease and aggregate\n    demand will decrease. The aggregate demand curve will shift from AD to AD1. The short run\n    equilibrium point will be E1. The output level will be lower than Y0, and the price level will be lower\n    than P0.\n    In the long run, the short run aggregate supply curve will shift from SRAS to SRAS1. The long run\n    equilibrium point will be E2. The output level will be Y1 and the price level will be P2, which is lower\n    than P1.",
