      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/43/q-p1-01.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/43/a-p1-01.jpg",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     Macy received a theatre ticket worth $280 from her father. The $280 was not paid by Macy herself, so it\n     does not constitute the opportunity cost of choosing to watch the play.\n     If Macy chooses to watch the play, she has to forgo the $300 income she could earn from taking a\n     part-time job at a fast-food restaurant. This $300 is the opportunity cost of choosing to watch the play.\n     At the same time, she also has to forgo the $200 income she could receive by selling the ticket to her\n     friend. This $200 is also the opportunity cost of choosing to watch the play.\n     Therefore, Macy’s opportunity cost of choosing to watch the play = $300 + $200 = $500.",
