      "questionTextEng": "A listed company in Hong Kong is considering issuing shares or bonds to raise capital. For the existing\n     shareholders, which of the following is an advantage of issuing shares instead of bonds?\n\n           A.     Since most small investors in Hong Kong participate in the stock market rather than the bond market,\n                  issuing shares can raise more capital.\n           B.     The company has no repayment obligation.\n           C.     Existing shareholders’ power of control over the company will not be diluted.\n           D.     If the share price rises, shareholders can earn capital gains.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     The company has no obligation to redeem shares from shareholders.\n     Option A is incorrect. The amount of capital raised by issuing shares is not necessarily greater than that\n     raised by issuing bonds.\n     Option C is incorrect. Since new shareholders may participate in company decision-making, issuing\n     shares will weaken the existing shareholders’ control over the company, whereas issuing bonds would\n     not.\n     Option D is incorrect. Regardless of whether the company issues shares or bonds, existing shareholders\n     can earn capital gains when the share price increases. This is irrelevant to the decision to raise capital\n     through issuing shares and is not an advantage of issuing shares over bonds.",
