      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows data for two firms:\n\n                                                                     Firm A                         Firm B\n                     Number of workers employed                        30                             25\n                    Working hours per worker/month                    160                            200\n                    Average output per worker/month                   512                            750\n                    Percentage of output with quality\n                                                                      1.1%                           1.3%\n                            below standard\n\n      Refer to the above table. Which of the following can best explain the difference in average labour productivity\n      and quality of outputs between the two firms?\n\n            A.    Firm B uses piece rate while firm A uses time rate.\n            B.    Firm A has a better working environment than firm B.\n            C.    Firm A employs workers with higher skills.\n            D.    Firm B does not provide appropriate training to its employees.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: A\n     Average labour productivity of firm A = (30 × 512) units of output / (30 × 160) man-hours = 3.2 units of\n     output/man-hour\n     Average labour productivity of firm B = (25 × 750) units of output / (25 × 200) man-hours = 3.75 units\n     of output/man-hour\n     Firm B’s workers have higher productivity, but the percentage of output with quality below standard is\n     also higher. Option A can explain the above difference. Firm B’s workers may rush for more output in\n     order to earn more income. In contrast, firm A’s workers have no incentive to rush for more output in\n    order to earn more income.",
