      "questionTextEng": "The table below shows the input-output relationship of a firm. The only input required for production is labour.\n      The fixed cost is $10 and the wage rate per unit of labour is $5.\n\n                                      Labour (units)             Total output (units)\n                                           2                              8\n                                           3                             13\n                                           4                             19\n                                           5                             26\n                                           6                             32\n\n      According to the above table, which of the following statements is CORRECT?\n\n            A.    Since the average cost for each unit of output keeps falling, the firm enjoys economies of scale.\n            B.    The optimal scale of production is 26 units.\n            C.    When the 6th unit of labour is employed, marginal product starts to diminish, indicating that the law\n                  of diminishing marginal returns applies to the firm.\n            D.    Since total output keeps rising, the law of diminishing marginal returns does not apply.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: C\n             Labour (units)           Total output (units)      Marginal product (units)\n                     2                          8                            -\n                     3                         13                            5\n                     4                         19                            6\n                     5                         26                            7\n                     6                         32                            6\n     Option C is correct. When the 6th unit of labour is employed, the marginal product of labour falls from\n     7 units to 6 units.\n     Options A and B are incorrect. Since the firm has fixed factors of production (fixed cost is $10), the firm\n    is operating in the short run. The concepts of economies of scale and optimal scale only apply in the\n    long run.",
