      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/44/a-p1-01.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following situations imply the existence of scarcity?\n\n           (1)   People have to make choices among options.\n           (2)   People are unable to obtain the goods or services they want even if they are willing to pay the price.\n           (3)   People have to pay a cost to obtain certain goods or services.\n\n           A.    (1) and (2) only\n           B.    (1) and (3) only\n           C.    (2) and (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n     (1) is correct. When people need to make choices among options, it means that the quantity of resources\n     is insufficient to satisfy all human wants and scarcity exists.\n     (2) is correct. When people are still unable to obtain the goods or services they want even if they are\n     willing to pay the price, it means a shortage exists. When a shortage exists, scarcity must also exist.\n     (3) is correct. The fact that people have to pay a cost to obtain certain goods or services means they are\n     economic goods. The quantity of these goods or services is insufficient to satisfy all human wants.",
