      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/44/q-p1-07.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/44/a-p1-07.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following may exist in a barter economy?\n\n           (1)   division of labour\n           (2)   private property rights\n           (3)   inflation\n\n           A.    (1) only\n           B.    (1) and (2) only\n           C.    (2) and (3) only\n           D.    (1), (2) and (3)",
      "answerEng": "Answer: B\n     (3) is incorrect. Inflation is a persistent increase in the general price level.\n     In a barter economy, there is no generally acceptable medium of exchange, so money does not exist. The\n     price of one good is expressed in terms of another good (relative price), rather than a monetary price. A\n     rise in the relative price of one good means a fall in the relative price of another good. Therefore,\n     inflation does not exist in a barter economy.",
      "originalAnswerImageEng": "originals/44/ae-p1-07.jpg",
      "originalQuestionImageEng": "originals/44/qe-p1-07.jpg",
