      "source": "模擬試卷四十四 卷一",
      "reviewedByAI": "Y",
      "lastReviewDate": "2026-09-24",
      "originalQuestionImage": "originals/44/q-p1-20.jpg",
      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/44/a-p1-20.jpg",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    When the government provides a per-unit subsidy for good X, supply will increase, leading to an\n    increase in quantity transacted and a decrease in price. At the same time, a decrease in the price of\n    substitutes for good X will reduce the demand for good X, causing both the quantity transacted and\n    price to decrease. To conclude, the price will decrease, and the marginal benefit of the last unit of good\n    X will decrease.\n    At the original equilibrium point, total social surplus (= consumer surplus + producer surplus) is\n    maximised. After the per-unit subsidy is provided, the total amount of the subsidy exceeds the increases\n    in consumer surplus and producer surplus. Part of the subsidy is neither gained by consumers nor\n    received by producers and becomes deadweight loss. Meanwhile, when the demand for good X\n    decreases, both producer surplus and consumer surplus will decrease. To conclude, the total social\n    surplus of good X will decrease.",
