      "originalAnswerImage": "originals/44/a-p1-26.jpg",
      "questionTextEng": "The per capita real GDP of a country has increased, but people generally think that their standard of living has\n      declined. Which of the following situations can best explain this phenomenon?\n\n            A.    There are more unreported economic activities in the country.\n            B.    The inflation rate of the country has risen.\n            C.    The population of the country has increased.\n            D.    The income distribution of the country has become more uneven.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    Even if a country’s per capita real GDP increases, if income distribution becomes more uneven, it means\n    a small number of rich people may consume more or higher-quality goods, while the majority may\n    consume fewer or lower-quality goods. Therefore, people will generally think that their standard of\n    living has declined.\n    Option A is incorrect. If there are more unreported economic activities in the market, people’s standard\n    of living will increase.\n    Options B and C are incorrect. Per capita real GDP has already eliminated the effects of changes in the\n    price level and population.",
