      "questionTextEng": "The diagram below shows the aggregate demand curve, short run aggregate supply curve and long run aggregate\n      supply curve for an economy.\n\n                               Price level\n                                             LRAS\n                                                                    SRAS\n\n                                 P0\n\n                                                                            AD\n                                 0                                                 Output level\n                                              Yf       Y0\n\n      Which of the following descriptions is CORRECT?\n\n              A.    In the long run, the price level will be lower than P 0.\n              B.    In the long run, the full-employment output level will rise to Y0.\n              C.    A reduction in the profits tax rate will help restore the output level to the full-employment output\n                    level.\n              D.    Through the market adjustment mechanism, the short run aggregate supply will decrease and the\n                    output level will be restored to the full-employment output level.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    There is an inflationary (output) gap in the economy. There is excess demand in the factor market,\n    resulting in an upward pressure on input prices. The cost of production will increase. Short run\n    aggregate supply will decrease until the output level returns to the full-employment output level Yf.\n    Option A is incorrect. The short run aggregate supply curve will shift to the left until it intersects with\n    the LRAS and AD. The price level will be higher than P0.\n\n    Option B is incorrect. The economy’s productive capacity has not changed, so the full-employment\n    output level will remain unchanged.\n    Option C is incorrect. A reduction in the profits tax rate will increase investment expenditure, causing\n    aggregate demand to increase and the output level to increase further, which will not help restore the\n    output level to the full-employment output level.",
