      "questionTextEng": "Suppose there are a fiscal surplus and a trade deficit in an economy. Which of the following may increase the fiscal\n    surplus and reduce the trade deficit?\n\n            A.    The government introduces a general sales tax.\n            B.    The government increases the quota for importing foreign labour.\n            C.    Households’ desire to save increases.\n            D.    The government imposes a tariff on imported goods.",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    When the government imposes a tariff on imported goods, imported goods become more expensive.\n    Imports will decrease, which will help reduce the trade deficit. At the same time, tariffs are a form of\n    government revenue and will therefore increase the fiscal surplus.\n    Option A is incorrect. The imposition of a general sales tax can increase government revenue and thus\n    increase its fiscal surplus. However, the prices of goods will rise and exports may decrease, which may\n    worsen the trade deficit.\n    Option B is incorrect. When the government increases the quota for importing foreign labour, the\n    increase in the employed population will lead to higher salaries tax revenue for the government, which\n    can increase its fiscal surplus. However, when short run aggregate supply increases, output will increase\n    and the total import value will increase, worsening the trade deficit.\n    Option C is incorrect. When households’ desire to save increases, they will reduce consumption and\n    total import value will decrease, helping to reduce the trade deficit. However, when private consumption\n    expenditure decreases, aggregate demand will decrease. The output level will decrease and government\n    tax revenue will decrease. The fiscal surplus will not increase.",
