      "questionTextEng": "Which of the following is an example of a contractionary monetary policy?\n\n            A.    a reduction in the basic allowance for the salaries tax\n            B.    an increase in loan interest rates by commercial banks\n            C.    a reduction in the discount rate\n            D.    an open market sale of government bonds by the central bank",
      "answerEng": "Answer: D\n    When the central bank carries out an open market sale of government bonds, it will reduce the money\n    supply. This is a contractionary monetary policy.\n    Option A is incorrect. Reducing the basic allowance for the salaries tax will cause people’s disposable\n    income to fall. Private consumption expenditure will decrease and aggregate demand will decrease. This\n    is a contractionary fiscal policy.\n    Option B is incorrect. Although commercial banks raising the loan interest rate will cause consumption\n    and investment to decrease, this is not a policy adopted by the central bank and therefore does not\n    constitute monetary policy.\n    Option C is incorrect. Lowering the discount rate will increase the money supply. This is an\n    expansionary monetary policy.",
