      "questionTextEng": "Housing has been an issue of public concern in Hong Kong.\nSource A: Basic infonnation about housing in Hong Kong\nPrivate residential units and public rental housing (PRH) units are the two major types of housing units in Hong Kong.\n        Private residential units        Public rental housing (PRH) units\nNumber of units        1 270 000        826 700\nAverage rental of a typical unit (about 300 square feet) in urban area        $14 00        $2 800\nMedian monthly household income        $40 300        $19 700\nEligibility in terms of income limit                Monthly income not exceeding\n$30 950 (for 4-person households)\n\nIn the fiscal year of 2022-23, it is estimated that the total rental value of private residential units contributed $300 billion to Hong Kong’s GDP.\n\nSource B: Average waiting time for public rental housing (PRH) units\nPeriod        Average waiting time* for public rental housing (PRH) units for general applicants\n2024 first quarter        5.7 years\n* Waiting time refers to the time taken between registration for public rental housing (PRH) and first flat offer, excluding any frozen period during the application period.\n \nSource C: Some discussions on public housing policy in a forum\nThe host: The Government decided to raise the monthly rental of public rental housing (PRH) units by 10%.\nA politician: We expect that the increase in monthly rental by 10% would raise public rental housing (PRH) tenants' total rental payment by 10%.\nA public rentaI housing(PRH) appIicant: I think the increase in monthly rental is going to have little effect on the waiting time for public rental housing (PRH) units.\n\nSource D: Some views on housing issues in Hong Kong\nAn owner of several private residential units\n         I expect to enjoy some capital gains if the Government reduces the land supply for the construction of private residential units.\n\nA property agent\n         Our business may face greater challenges if the housing policy of Hong Kong becomes more reliant on public rental housing (PRH) units instead of private residential units.\n\n \n(a)        Refer to Source A.\n(i)        Only price competition is involved in the allocation of public rental housing (PRH) units. Do you agree? Explain your answer based on Source A.        (2 marks)\n(ii)        Give ONE reason to explain why tenants' total rental payment on private residential units in Hong Kong would be less than the contribution of the total rental value of the private residential units to GDP of $300 billion.        (2 marks)\n(b)        Refer to Sources B and C.\n(i)        Illustrate the economic phenomenon revealed by Source B with a supply-demand diagram.\n(ii)        With the aid of the same diagram in (b)(i), explain under what condition(s) the politician's comment in Source C about PRH tenants' total rental payment is correct.\n                (6 marks)\n(c)        Refer to Source C. Justify the PRH applicant's comment about the waiting time for PRH units.        (4 marks)\nFor part (d), candidates are required to present their answers in an essay form. Criteria for marking will include the use of the sources and economic theories, relevant content, logical presentation and clarity of expression.\n(d)\tSuppose the Government has the following two policy objectives.\nObjective 1: to reduce the waiting time for PRH units\nObjective 2: to reduce income inequality\nConsider the following two strategies.\nStrategy X\tIncreasing the penalty for PRH tenants whose incomes substantially exceed the limit\nStrategy Y\tAllocating more land for the construction of PRH units instead of private residential units\n\nWith reference to the above sources and your own knowledge in Economics,\n-\tdiscuss how EACH of the two strategies may help achieve EACH policy objective, and\n-\texcept for the effects on PRH tenants, discuss ONE possible undesirable economic effect of Strategy X on one group of stakeholders and ONE possible undesirable economic effect of Strategy Y on another group of stakeholders.\t(14 marks)",
