      "questionTextChi": "Figure 5 shows the production possibility frontier (PPF_A) of Country A. The Point N is the production point when there is no trade. AA’ is the tangent to the PPF_A at N.\n\nFigure 5\n(A graph is shown with \"Good Y\" on the y-axis and \"Good X\" on the x-axis. A concave curve labeled PPF_A represents the production possibility frontier. A dashed straight line labeled AA' is tangent to the PPF_A curve at Point N. Point A is on the y-axis at 20, and Point A' is on the x-axis at 20. Point Z is located on the dashed line AA', outside the PPF_A curve.)\n\n(a) Explain whether it is possible for Country A to produce at Point Z. (2 marks)\n\n(b) Initially, Country A did not engage in international trade. Indicate in Figure 5 the consumption point of Country A as C_N. (1 mark)\n\n(c) Country A opens up to international trade and the world price per unit of good X is 1/3 units of good Y.\n\n(i) Explain which good Country A will export. (2 marks)\n(ii) Indicate in Figure 5 the new production point of Country A as P_A. (1 mark)\n(iii) Indicate in Figure 5 the new consumption possibility frontier of Country A after trade as CPF_A. (1 mark)\n(iv) Suppose Country A consumes the same amount of good Y before trade and after trade. Indicate in Figure 5 Country A’s new consumption point, the import volume and the export volume as C_T, M and X respectively. (3 marks)\n\n(d) Suppose an import quota is imposed on Country A by other countries. Suggest ONE possible reason to explain why the above quota will negatively affect Country A’s economic growth in the long run. (2 marks)\n\n(e) Suggest ONE policy that the government of Country A could adopt to raise the country’s Human Development Index in the following two dimensions respectively.\n\n(i) education (2 marks)\n(ii) health (2 marks)",
